/// \defgroup grp_w2_wwlib_string_mem WWLib StringClass, the global new and delete, and the CPU speed probe
/// \ingroup grp_wwlib_thirdparty
/// \brief Westwood's narrow-character string class, the four global
///        allocation operators that hand global new and delete to the
///        game's memory pool, and the RDTSC-based CPU clock estimate.
///
/// Three small pieces of WWLib:
/// - StringClass's out-of-line members, which Zero Hour keeps in
///   wwstring.cpp, follow Zero Hour's code, with two BFME 2 differences,
///   both in the memory calls: string buffers come from the tagged array
///   operator new with the multi-character id 'str' (a four-byte value),
///   which selects the memory pool's string heap, and
///   StringClass::Free_String releases them through scalar operator delete
///   (memory class 1) rather than array delete. StringClass::Get_String and
///   StringClass::Free_String, which manage the temporary buffers, are
///   described with the class but have no entries of their own on this
///   page. Each member's ledger row names the source unit that holds it.
/// - mem_ops.cpp holds the global operator new, operator new[], operator
///   delete and operator delete[]. They do no work of their own. Each calls
///   through one of two function pointers into the exported MemoryPool API
///   (see \ref sub_memory). Zero Hour's GameMemory.cpp is not their source.
/// - Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed.cpp holds Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed, which times the
///   processor's time-stamp counter against the high-resolution performance
///   counter. Its body is the same as BFME 1's and differs from Zero Hour's
///   mpu.cpp version.
///
/// StringClass serves the WWVegas side of the engine. Its users include W3D
/// device enumeration and capability logs, the WWLib file factory, registry
/// access and CPU detection. Game-engine code has its own string classes
/// (Zero Hour: AsciiString and UnicodeString); see \ref sub_common_services.
///
/// The callers named on this page come from the project's call-graph tool.
/// To see a function's current ledger row, including its source unit, and
/// its callers, use the commands in \ref page_reading_code_lookup.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is data-driven. No INI field, map script or asset
///   changes how these functions behave.
/// - StringClass::Format and StringClass::Format_Args format into a fixed
///   512-character stack buffer. Output of 512 characters or more is not
///   safely truncated: the buffer is left without a terminator, and the
///   copy into the string that follows reads past its end, so the string
///   can pick up stray stack bytes after the 512th character (see
///   StringClass::Format). Formatted text, including any file name it
///   carries, must stay under 512 characters. One user is the default
///   WWLib file factory: SimpleFileFactoryClass::Get_File uses Format to
///   put a search directory in front of a relative file name.
///   W3DDisplay::init constructs W3DFileSystem, which installs itself as
///   the WWLib file factory in place of that default.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The global operators are the memory seam. Global new and delete reach
///   the memory pool through two pointers that are looked up by export name
///   at run time, so a replacement allocator can be put behind them without
///   touching the call sites. The two pointers start out null and the
///   operators do not check them.
/// - A replacement allocator must accept frees whose memory class differs
///   from the allocation's. StringClass allocates its buffers through the
///   tagged array new (memory class 2), and retail StringClass::Free_String
///   releases them through scalar operator delete (memory class 1). Retail
///   MemoryPool::_Free ignores the class and finds the owning heap itself,
///   so the mismatch does no harm there.
/// - StringClass is narrow-character only in this build. See StringClass
///   for what a _UNICODE build would have to change.
/// - Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed's estimate is unreliable on modern processors; see
///   its own entry.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/wwstring.h
/// \ingroup grp_w2_wwlib_string_mem
/// \brief WWLib's StringClass declaration and its inline members.
///
/// The copy listed here is BFME 2's own header, in
/// Code/Libraries/Source/WWVegas/WWLib. It is modelled on Zero Hour's
/// wwstring.h, with the same object layout and inline bodies, but it need
/// not declare every member of Zero Hour's class, which also has, among
/// others, comparison operators, constructors and assignment operators
/// taking a TCHAR or a wide string, Erase and Trim. A member missing from
/// this header is therefore not evidence that retail lacks it; read Zero
/// Hour's wwstring.h for the full class.
///
/// Retail data confirms the header's original name: WorldBuilder's debug
/// build reports an assertion in StringClass::Set_Buffer_And_Allocated_Length
/// against the file wwstring.h under WWLib.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/mem_ops.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_wwlib_string_mem
/// \brief The global operator new, operator new[], operator delete and
///        operator delete[], each a one-call forwarder to the game's memory
///        pool.
///
/// The unit also defines the two pointers the operators call through: one
/// to an allocate function taking a size, a memory class and a heap id,
/// and one to a free function taking a block and a memory class. Both start
/// null. A memory-pool start-up routine fills them, together with the other
/// MemoryPool entry points, by looking up the exported names
/// MemoryPool::_Allocate and MemoryPool::_Free in the process's main module
/// with GetModuleHandleA and GetProcAddress. game.dat exports both names
/// itself.
///
/// The memory classes passed by retail callers include 1 for scalar new
/// and delete, 2 for the array forms (including the tagged array operator
/// new that StringClass and nstrdup use), and 3 for the C library free that
/// BFME 2 replaces. Retail MemoryPool::_Allocate and MemoryPool::_Free do
/// not use the class: _Allocate chooses the heap from the heap id, and
/// _Free finds the heap that owns the block.
///
/// Zero Hour: GameMemory.cpp defines these operators instead. They
/// initialise the memory manager on first use and pass each request to
/// TheDynamicMemoryAllocator. BFME 2's operators do neither.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w2_wwlib_string_mem
/// \brief Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed: estimates the processor clock in MHz.
///
/// This unit holds a single function. Open-BFME-1 reconstructed the same
/// body for BFME 1, and its compiled bytes are found unchanged in BFME 2.
/// Zero Hour declares the function in MPU.H and defines a different, more
/// careful version in mpu.cpp.

/// \class StringClass
/// \ingroup grp_w2_wwlib_string_mem
/// \brief Westwood's growable, heap-backed C string: one pointer to the
///        characters, with the buffer's capacity and cached length stored
///        in a small header just before them.
///
/// A StringClass object holds only a pointer to null-terminated TCHAR
/// characters, so it converts to const TCHAR * at no cost and is the size
/// of a pointer. Every buffer other than the shared empty string has a
/// two-int header, directly before the characters, that records:
/// - the allocated length, in characters, including room for the
///   terminator;
/// - the cached string length. Zero means "not known": Get_Length then
///   counts the characters with strlen and caches the result.
///
/// An empty string with no buffer points at a shared static null character
/// (m_EmptyString points at m_NullChar) and has no header. Every helper
/// that reads or writes the header checks for that case first.
///
/// \par Ownership and lifetime
/// - Each object owns its buffer. Copying and assignment copy the
///   characters. Buffers are never shared or reference-counted.
/// - Constructors that take hint_temporary set to true ask
///   StringClass::Get_String for one of a small set of static temporary
///   buffers instead of a heap block. StringClass's static members hold
///   that pool: eight blocks of 256 bytes, header included, a bit mask
///   recording which are in use, and a lock guarding the mask. A request
///   that does not fit, or finds every block taken, gets a heap buffer of
///   the requested length (or the shared empty string when that length is
///   0). The operator+ overloads build their results this way.
/// - StringClass::Free_String, called by the destructor and before any
///   buffer is replaced, returns a temporary block to the pool or deletes a
///   heap buffer, and leaves the object pointing at the empty string.
/// - Heap buffers come from the tagged array operator new with the id
///   'str'. BFME 2's memory-pool start-up code registers a heap under that
///   id, so string buffers live in their own heap. Zero Hour's release
///   build allocates them with plain new[].
///
/// \par Private helpers
/// This documentation is built with EXTRACT_PRIVATE=NO, so the private
/// members' own blocks are not rendered. In short:
/// - Allocate_Buffer takes a block from the string heap, writes its header
///   and returns the character pointer. No terminator is written.
/// - Set_Buffer_And_Allocated_Length frees the current buffer, installs a
///   new one and records its capacity, with the cached length reset to
///   unknown.
/// - Resize grows the buffer and keeps the contents (the operator+=
///   overloads use it). Uninitialised_Grow grows it without copying and
///   always resets the cached length (the StringClass and const TCHAR *
///   assignment operators, Get_Buffer and Copy_Wide use it).
/// - Both grow to exactly the size requested, never geometrically, and
///   neither shrinks a buffer. Because growth is exact rather than
///   geometric, once a string has filled its buffer, every further append
///   reallocates and copies the whole string. Reserving capacity first
///   avoids this: the int constructor reserves at least the requested
///   capacity and starts with an empty string. Get_Buffer also grows the
///   buffer, but when it grows it the old contents are discarded and no
///   terminator is written, so the caller must write the whole string.
/// - Get_String and Free_String manage the temporary pool described above.
///   They have no entries of their own on this page.
///
/// \par Thread safety
/// Only the temporary-pool bookkeeping is locked. A StringClass object
/// itself must not be used from two threads at once.
///
/// \par Inline members in retail
/// Several members that the header defines inline also exist in retail as
/// out-of-line copies, among them the StringClass and const TCHAR *
/// assignment operators, the C-string and (int, bool) constructors, the
/// three operator+= overloads, Get_Buffer, StringClass::Allocate_Buffer and
/// StringClass::Set_Buffer_And_Allocated_Length. Other call sites expand
/// some of the same members inline, so hooking or patching one out-of-line
/// copy does not catch every use.
///
/// \par BFME 2 compared with Zero Hour
/// The members documented on this page, and Get_String and Free_String,
/// follow Zero Hour's logic. The differences are the string heap and the
/// scalar delete used to free buffers.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// TCHAR is char in this build. Defining _UNICODE would switch Format and
/// Format_Args to _vsnwprintf, but Copy_Wide has no wide-character path: it
/// passes Get_Buffer's TCHAR pointer to WideCharToMultiByte as the narrow
/// output buffer. It is only correct while TCHAR is char, so a _UNICODE
/// port has to rewrite it. WWLib already has WideStringClass for wide
/// text.
/// \see StringClass::Format, StringClass::Format_Args, StringClass::Copy_Wide

/// \fn TCHAR *StringClass::Allocate_Buffer(int length)
/// \brief Allocates a heap buffer for length characters plus the header,
///        fills in the header, and returns a pointer to the characters.
///
/// The header records length as the allocated length and 0 (unknown) as
/// the string length. The characters themselves are left uninitialised,
/// so no terminator is written.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The block comes from the tagged array operator new, which BFME 2 passes
/// the size and the string heap's id 'str'. The result is not checked for
/// null. Retail has an out-of-line copy of this function, and
/// StringClass::Resize, StringClass::Uninitialised_Grow and
/// StringClass::Get_String expand it inline: they call the tagged array
/// operator new themselves.
///
/// \param length Capacity in characters, which must include the terminator
///               the caller will write.
/// \return Pointer to the first character, just past the header. Release
///         it only through StringClass::Free_String, never directly.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringClass::Set_Buffer_And_Allocated_Length

/// \fn void StringClass::Set_Buffer_And_Allocated_Length(TCHAR *buffer, int length)
/// \brief Frees the current buffer, installs a new one, and records its
///        capacity.
///
/// StringClass::Free_String runs first, so the old contents are gone even
/// when they were wanted. Copying them is the caller's job, done before
/// this call, as StringClass::Resize does. The cached length is reset to 0
/// (unknown), because the new buffer's contents are not necessarily
/// defined. When buffer is the shared empty string, there is no header and
/// nothing is recorded.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Retail has an out-of-line copy of this function, and inline expansions
/// of it in callers that include StringClass::Resize,
/// StringClass::Uninitialised_Grow and StringClass::Get_String.
/// WorldBuilder's debug build asserts that length is 0 when buffer is the
/// empty string. The release build has no such check.
///
/// \param buffer The new character pointer, normally from
///               StringClass::Allocate_Buffer. The object takes ownership.
/// \param length The capacity of buffer, in characters.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringClass::Allocate_Buffer

/// \fn void StringClass::Resize(int size)
/// \brief Makes sure the buffer can hold size characters, keeping the
///        current contents.
///
/// The three operator+= overloads call it before appending. When the
/// allocated length is already at least size, nothing happens. Otherwise a
/// new buffer of exactly size characters is allocated, the old string is
/// copied into it with strcpy, and it replaces the old buffer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// After a reallocation the cached length is 0 (unknown), so callers store
/// the new length themselves, as operator+= does. Because growth is exact
/// rather than geometric, once a string has filled its buffer, every
/// further append reallocates and copies the whole string. Reserving
/// capacity first, for example with the int constructor, avoids this.
///
/// \param size Required capacity in characters, including the terminator.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringClass::Uninitialised_Grow, StringClass::Allocate_Buffer

/// \fn void StringClass::Uninitialised_Grow(int length)
/// \brief Makes sure the buffer can hold length characters without keeping
///        its contents, and marks the cached length as unknown.
///
/// Used where the caller is about to overwrite the whole string. Its
/// callers include the StringClass and const TCHAR * assignment operators,
/// Get_Buffer, StringClass::Copy_Wide, RegistryClass::Get_String,
/// RegistryClass::Get_Value_List, DX8Wrapper::Enumerate_Devices,
/// CPUDetectClass::Init_OS and Filename_From_Asset_Name.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// A buffer that is already large enough is kept as it is, characters
/// included. A smaller one is replaced by a new buffer of exactly length
/// characters, and the old contents are discarded, not copied. In both
/// cases the cached length is then set to 0, so the caller writes the
/// characters and stores the real length.
///
/// \param length Required capacity in characters, including the terminator.
/// \note With length 0 on an empty string, no buffer is allocated and the
///       object keeps pointing at the shared empty string, which must not
///       be written to.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see StringClass::Resize

/// \fn int StringClass::Format(const TCHAR *format, ...)
/// \brief printf-style formatting into the string, replacing its contents.
///
/// Its callers are mostly W3D diagnostics and naming. They include the
/// DX8Wrapper render-state and texture-stage name helpers,
/// DX8Wrapper::Apply_Default_State, DX8Caps::Compute_Caps,
/// DX8PolygonRendererClass::Log, the CPUDetectClass log builders,
/// FontCharsClass::Initialize_GDI_Font (the font name and point size) and
/// SimpleFileFactoryClass::Get_File (a search directory plus a file name).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The arguments are formatted with _vsnprintf into a zero-filled
/// 512-character stack buffer, which is then assigned to the string. The
/// code is a copy of StringClass::Format_Args with its own va_list; it does
/// not call Format_Args.
///
/// \param format A printf format string.
/// \return _vsnprintf's result: the number of characters written, or -1
///         when the output did not fit.
/// \warning Output of 512 characters or more fills the whole buffer, and
///          MSVC's _vsnprintf then writes no terminator, so the assignment
///          that follows can read past the end of the stack buffer. A port
///          to a C99 vsnprintf would always terminate, cutting the text at
///          511 characters, and would also change the return value, which
///          then reports the untruncated length.
/// \see StringClass::Format_Args

/// \fn int StringClass::Format_Args(const TCHAR *format, const va_list &arg_list)
/// \brief Formats an existing argument list into the string, replacing its
///        contents.
///
/// This is the form for wrappers that receive their own variable arguments.
/// It behaves exactly like StringClass::Format, with the same 512-character
/// buffer and the same hazard: output of 512 characters or more leaves the
/// buffer unterminated before it is copied.
///
/// \param format   A printf format string.
/// \param arg_list The arguments, already started with va_start by the
///                 caller. The caller also calls va_end.
/// \return _vsnprintf's result: the number of characters written, or -1
///         when the output did not fit.
/// \see StringClass::Format

/// \fn bool StringClass::Copy_Wide(const WCHAR *source)
/// \brief Replaces the string with a narrow copy of a wide string,
///        converted through the system ANSI code page.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// WideCharToMultiByte is called twice with the code page CP_ACP: first to
/// size the result and learn whether every character could be mapped, then
/// to convert into a buffer from Get_Buffer. The stored length is the
/// converted length without the terminator. When the sizing call returns 0,
/// the string is left as it was.
///
/// \param source The null-terminated wide string. Null is allowed.
/// \return True when every character had an equivalent in the ANSI code
///         page. False when source is null, or when some character was
///         replaced by the code page's default character.
/// \note The result depends on the Windows system locale, so the same text
///       can convert differently on different machines.
/// \warning If the sizing call fails, the return value comes from a local
///          flag that is never initialised and that the failed call is not
///          documented to set, so it is unpredictable.

/// \fn void *operator new(unsigned int size)
/// \brief Global scalar new: asks the game's memory pool for size bytes
///        with memory class 1 and heap id 0.
///
/// A new expression for a type without its own operator new ends here.
/// Its callers include CreateGameEngine, WinMain, STLport's stream set-up,
/// GameEngine::createFileSystem and W3DDisplay::init. Heap id 0 leaves the
/// choice of heap to MemoryPool::_Allocate, which in retail uses the
/// calling thread's current heap when one is set and the default heap
/// otherwise (see \ref sub_memory).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// One indirect call through the allocate pointer defined in mem_ops.cpp.
/// Neither the pointer nor the returned block is checked, so a call made
/// before the pointer is bound crashes. Whether the pool reports failure by
/// returning null or otherwise is not documented here.
///
/// \param size Number of bytes requested.
/// \return The new block.
/// \see operator delete(void *), operator new[](unsigned int)

/// \fn void *operator new[](unsigned int size)
/// \brief Global array new: asks the game's memory pool for size bytes with
///        memory class 2 and heap id 0.
///
/// The same forwarder as the scalar form, apart from the memory class.
/// The tagged array operator new is one way to choose a heap: it takes a
/// heap id as a second argument. Its callers include StringClass's buffer
/// allocation and nstrdup, which pass 'str'. Other code instead sets the
/// calling thread's current heap, which heap id 0 then selects (see
/// operator new(unsigned int)).
///
/// \param size Number of bytes requested.
/// \return The new block.
/// \see operator delete[](void *), operator new(unsigned int)

/// \fn void operator delete(void *block)
/// \brief Global scalar delete: returns a block to the game's memory pool
///        with memory class 1. A null block is ignored.
///
/// Its callers include scalar and vector deleting destructors throughout
/// game.dat, and StringClass::Free_String, which releases string buffers
/// allocated as arrays.
///
/// \param block The block to free, or null.
/// \see operator new(unsigned int), operator delete[](void *)

/// \fn void operator delete[](void *block)
/// \brief Global array delete: returns a block to the game's memory pool
///        with memory class 2. A null block is ignored.
///
/// Its callers include vector deleting destructors, the WWLib VectorClass
/// and DynamicVectorClass containers (including their StringClass
/// instantiations), STLport's file buffers and MilesAudioManager's loop
/// buffers.
///
/// \param block The block to free, or null.
/// \see operator new[](unsigned int), operator delete(void *)

/// \fn int Get_RDTSC_CPU_Speed(void)
/// \brief Estimates the processor clock speed in MHz, rounded to a
///        common processor clock step.
///
/// What BFME 2 does with the value is not established here. Zero Hour:
/// Dump_Exception_Info prints the value in the crash report written when
/// the game faults.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Without a high-resolution performance counter
///   (QueryPerformanceFrequency fails) the measurement is skipped and the
///   function returns 0.
/// - Otherwise one sample is taken. The performance counter is read, then
///   CPUID (to serialise the processor) and RDTSC read the time-stamp
///   counter. The code busy-waits until timeGetTime has advanced by at
///   least 5 milliseconds, then reads both counters again.
/// - The speed is the time-stamp cycles divided by the elapsed time, using
///   only the low 32 bits of each counter, scaled to MHz.
/// - The raw value is snapped to the nearer of the closest multiple of
///   50 MHz and the closest multiple of 200/3 MHz (the 66, 133, 200, 266
///   and 333 MHz family). A tie goes to the 200/3 MHz step, and a result of
///   666 is returned as 667.
///
/// \return The estimated clock in MHz, or 0 when no performance counter is
///         available.
/// \note Zero Hour's mpu.cpp version is more careful: it raises the process
///       and thread priority, repeats the measurement at least 3 and at
///       most 20 times until the last three readings each lie within 1 MHz
///       of their average, and does not snap the result.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Treat the value as a rough label, not a measurement. On processors
///   with an invariant time-stamp counter it reports the counter's fixed
///   rate rather than the current clock. On processors whose counter
///   follows the clock, it reports whatever power state was active during
///   the sample.
/// - The thread is neither pinned to one core nor raised in priority, so
///   the two RDTSC reads can come from different cores, and a preempted
///   sample is not retried.
/// - The 5 millisecond wait depends on timeGetTime's granularity, which
///   follows the system timer resolution, so the real sampling interval
///   varies between machines.
/// - The snapping to 50 and 200/3 MHz steps suits processors of the game's
///   era, not today's clock speeds.
